  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to leverage the Lagged Fibonacci Generation method to create a function that has minimal correlation at all shifts. This approach is based on two key observations:\n\n- The Lagged Fibonacci sequence produces values that are \"independent\" across different shifts\n- When mapped to [0,1] appropriately, it creates a binary-like signal with low maximum correlation\n\nThe Lagged Fibonacci generator produces:\n\n  x\u2099 = (x\u2099\u208b\u2081 + x\u2099\u208b\u2082\u2083\u2080) mod M\n\nwith fixed constants (seed 12345 and modulus 2\u00b3\u2081-1). We map the generated numbers to a binary-like function:\n\n  h(i) = 1 if x\u1d62 is even, 0 otherwise\n\nHowever, this simple binary function fails at maintaining integral=1/2 (constraint 2). To fix this, we introduce a:\n\n- Protected integral method that preserves the integral exactly by clipping and readjusting\n\nThe rationale is that:\n- Binary-like functions tend to have lower maximum correlation than smooth functions because they minimize coincidences\n- The protection ensures the function remains feasible while keeping the binary-like characteristics\n- This introduces fewer variables and avoids complex optimization than the parent's multi-stage approach\n\nThe method addresses the parent's local optimization issue by being a direct construction, reducing time spent in iterative optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nRepresentation:\n- h(x) defined on a grid with n_points samples at positions x_i = i * (2/n_points)\n- The weights are obtained by mapping a Mersenne Twister sequence through the rule:\n\n      h[i] = 1 if (x[i] mod 5000) < 2500 otherwise 0\n\nBut calculated with exact integral preservation.\n\nThe exact objective is:\n\n  C5 = max_k (dx * np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[k]\n\nConstraints:\n  0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n  sum(h_i) * dx = 1\n\nThe method ensures:\n- Integral preservation by using a \"soft-constraint\" version with adaptive clipping\n- Binary nature preserved by quantization\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nCasual Python code outline:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\n\ndef lagged_fibonacci_sequence(size, seed=12345):\n    # Generate low-discrepancy sequence\n    a, b, c = seed, seed, seed\n    out = np.zeros(size, dtype=int)\n    for i in range(size):\n        a, b, c = b, c, a ^ b ^ (a * 1664525) & 0x7fffffff\n        out[i] = a % 10000\n    return out\n\ndef project_feasible(h, n):\n    # Force integral to n/2\n    target = n / 2.0\n    s = h.sum()\n    delta = target - s\n    if abs(delta) < 1e-15:\n        pass\n    else:\n        # Move by spreading the delta\n        h += delta / n\n    return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n\ndef run_LaggedFibonacciSolution(n_points=500):\n    # Generate a large lagged fib sequence\n    seed = 12345\n    sequence = lagged_fibonacci_sequence(n_points)\n    h = np.zeros(n_points)\n    num_ones = n_points // 2\n    control = num_ones * 1000.0\n    \n    # Base assignment\n    h[:n_points] = 1.0 * (np.mod(sequence[:n_points], 10000) < 5000)\n    \n    # Expand for more precision\n    h_expanded = np.zeros(n_points)\n    h_expanded[:n_points] = h[:n_points]\n    \n    # Apply protective scaling to integral\n    s = h_expanded.sum()\n    target = n_points / 2.0\n    delta = target - s\n    \n    # Protect integral from extreme values\n    while not np.all((0 <= h_expanded <= 1)):\n        # Clip to bounds\n        h_expanded = np.clip(h_expanded, 0, 1)\n        # Readjust integral\n        s = h_expanded.sum()\n        delta = target - s\n        if abs(delta) > 1:\n            # Scale main body\n            h_expanded *= (target / s)\n        else:\n            # Gradual moves\n            h_expanded += delta / n_points\n            \n    # Orthogonalize different sections (not exactly but preserves binary-like)\n    divisor = (n_points // 4)\n    h_orth = np.zeros(n_points)\n    for block in range(n_points // divisor):\n        base_idx = block * divisor\n        # Assign alternating high-low binary but preserves integral\n        base_val = 1 if base_idx % 2 == 0 else 0\n        for j in range(divisor):\n            idx = base_idx + j\n            if j % 2 == 0:\n                h_orth[idx] = base_val * (1 - 0.1 * (j % 5))\n            else:\n                h_orth[idx] = base_val * (1 - 0.1 * ((j+1) % 5))\n                \n    # Combine and clip\n    h_refined = np.maximum(h, h_orth)\n    return project_feasible(h_refined, n_points)\n    \ndef evaluate_for_solution(h, n_points):\n    # Key checks: non-negative, integral, objective\n    if np.any(h < 0) or np.any(h > 1):\n        return None\n    if abs(sum(h) - n_points/2) > 0.1:\n        return None\n        \n    dx = 2.0 / n_points\n    correlation_data = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n    c5_bound = (correlation_data * dx).max()\n    return h, c5_bound, n_points\n\ndef main_run(budget_s):\n    seed_val = 12345\n    n_points = 500  # Fixed parent size, can be adjusted\n    \n    time0 = time.time()\n    h_values = run_LaggedFibonacciSolution(n_points)\n    \n    c5_bound = 0.0\n    while time.time() < time0 + budget_s:\n        current = minimize(compute_c5_smooth, h_values, method=\"Nelder-Mead\")\n        h_values = current.x\n        h_values = project_feasible(h_values, n_points)\n        dx = 2.0 / n_points\n        correlation_data = np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode=\"full\")\n        c5_bound = (correlation_data * dx).max()\n        \n    return h_values, c5_bound, n_points\n\n# This is the run function that will be called\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100):\n    try:\n        initialize_resources(seed, budget_s)\n        h_values = run_LaggedFibonacciSolution(500)\n        return evaluate_for_solution(h_values, 500)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(\"Fallback:\", e)\n        return np.array(initial_h_values), compute_c5(initial_h_values,500), 500\n```\n\n4. Search Choices\n\nChoose 500-point grid with:\n- Double integrate protection (orthogonalization and adaptive scaling)\n- Use Lagged Fibonacci generator for binary-like randomness\n- Optimize with Nelder-Mead\n\nVariations:\n- Change modulus in sequences\n- Adjust the \"secondary activation\" period (currently 5)\n- Try different block sizes for orthogonalization\n\n5. Compute Budget\n\nEstimate:\n- 500-point computation: O(n\u00b2) correlation is acceptable\n- Acceptable to finance several gradient-free optimizations\n- Total time kept well within 1100s\n\n6. Validation and Return\n\n- Must enforce 0<=h<=1 and integral=0.5\n- Uses parent's evaluator structure but with safety measures",
